questions.question_text:
Between which two consecutive whole numbers does $\sqrt{13}$ lie, and is it a surd?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-0.5,0)--(6.5,0);
\foreach \x in {0,1,2,3,4,5,6} {\draw (\x,0.13)--(\x,-0.13); \node[below] at (\x,-0.15) {$\x$};}
\foreach \x in {0.5,1.5,2.5,3.5,4.5,5.5} {\draw (\x,0.07)--(\x,-0.07);}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

questions.solution_text:
$3^{2}=9$ and $4^{2}=16$, and $9<13<16$, so $\sqrt{13}$ lies between $3$ and $4$.
No whole number squares to give $13$, so $13$ is not a perfect square and the root is irrational.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-0.5,0)--(6.5,0);
\foreach \x in {0,1,2,3,4,5,6} {\draw (\x,0.13)--(\x,-0.13); \node[below] at (\x,-0.15) {$\x$};}
\foreach \x in {0.5,1.5,2.5,3.5,4.5,5.5} {\draw (\x,0.07)--(\x,-0.07);}
\fill (3.61,0) circle (2.2pt);
\node[above] at (3.61,0.18) {$\sqrt{13}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
